// Flame graph input for one profiling session.
//
// The viewer builds the tree client-side from folded strings; DQL's job is to
// return the smallest set of (path, weight) pairs that reconstructs it. Building
// the tree in DQL would mean recursive splitting -- awkward, and expensive on a
// signal billed by bytes scanned.
//
// Measured on a real session: 400 rows, 40.9 MB scanned, 51 ms, and those 400
// stacks carry 99.94% of total sample weight (2,802,250 of 2,803,906). The limit
// is therefore not meaningfully lossy.
//
// Two things that will waste your afternoon if you do not know them:
//   - takeFirst() will not wrap an expression. takeFirst(toLong(x)) returns ZERO
//     ROWS rather than erroring.
//   - dtctl spills results over 50 KB to a file, so `result.records` is empty and
//     `result.path` holds the data. Use --spill=never when reading it inline.
//
// IMPORTANT for interpretation: EventPipe's SampleProfiler is a WALL-CLOCK sampler.
// It samples every thread, including parked ones, so idle thread-pool threads
// dominate raw weight -- on the reference session the top stack was
// LowLevelLifoSemaphore.WaitNative at 28% of all samples, doing nothing at all.
// A flame graph rendered from unfiltered weight shows "waiting" as the hotspot and
// buries the real work. See idle-filter below.

fetch logs, from:-3h
| filter profile.session_id == "$SESSION_ID"
| filter isNotNull(profile.stack.folded)
| summarize samples = sum(toLong(profile.sample_count)),
            folded  = takeFirst(profile.stack.folded),
            source  = takeFirst(profile.source),
            kind    = takeFirst(profile.event_type),
            by:{profile.stack.hash}
| sort samples desc
| limit 400
